- op: discuss  # 39f3
  at: '2026-08-29T07:17:15Z'  # 39f3
  by: agent  # 39f3
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 39f3
  clock: 4  # 39f3
  nonce: 39f3  # 39f3
  message: "RULED — ADR-0049, 'A Deferred Crossing Is Disclosed, Not Minted' (extends ADR-0016). Accepted 2026-08-29.\n\nWHAT THIS ITEM ASKED FOR AND WHAT IT GOT. The ask was one adjudication for the whole family instead of nine implicit ones, with two branches offered: (a) a launch call IS a receive, or (b) it is not and all rows move together. The ruling is neither branch as framed, and the reason is that both assumed the choice was between minting a source and having nothing. ADR-0016 already ruled the OUTBOUND twin of this question five months ago -- INV-vavup forbids attributing a launched program's I/O to the launcher, and installs command_launch to DISCLOSE a crossing rather than resolve it. Server launch is that same mis-attribution pointed inward. There is a third state, it is already ratified in this tree, and this item's framing did not have it.\n\nTHE RULING. A boundary tag asserts the crossing happens AT THAT CALL, IN THAT CALL'S SCOPE. The discriminator: does the call return -- or write into a caller-visible location -- a value whose content is chosen by the party on the far side? Yes => a TRANSFER. No, and the call merely opens/registers/subscribes/schedules/defers => a DEFERRED CROSSING, which is disclosed and never minted. Not new law: it is exactly what removed Haskell's gracefulClose ('returns unit, so no byte reaches the program') and what WI-dosov applied when it dropped socket/bind/listen while keeping accept. ADR-0049 states the rule those were instances of.\n\nTHREE THINGS THIS ITEM GOT WRONG, ALL CORRECTED IN THE ADR.\n\n1. THE CENSUS. This item claims 37 rows / 9 languages. Its own enumeration sums to 52. Two independent re-counts under different membership rules produced 63 and 75/13. Four numbers, four answers -- because there was no written membership rule to count against, so every sweep invented one. Confirmed missing from the item's list: erlang (gen_tcp.accept, ssl.transport_accept, httpd.start), rust (TcpListener.accept), c/cpp (sys/socket.accept), go (echo.Echo.Start/StartTLS). That is the hardcoded-9-languages defect this item cites INV-kanuk for, recurring inside the item itself. Filed as WI-hazop.\n\n2. 'DO NOT ADJUDICATE A SUBSET' WAS RIGHT ABOUT CONVENIENCE-SPLITTING AND WRONG ABOUT THIS FAMILY. It is four mechanisms, not one: setup (socket/bind/listen), blocking serve loop (ListenAndServe/serve_forever/Warp.run), per-connection accept, and route-registration DSL (Phoenix.Router). Under the discriminator they separate cleanly, and ACCEPT IS NOT A MEMBER -- it returns a peer-chosen address to its caller, so it is a transfer and stays net_recv on its own merits. Ruling the four together would have taken accept with them, which is the env_read error INV-tutar closed. accept is now asserted separately across seven languages, explicitly because the launch rows and accept survive for OPPOSITE reasons.\n\n3. THE HEADLINE PYTHON EXAMPLE IS INERT. flask.Flask.run is in python's ambiguous_names; a bare app.run() yields ZERO edges and total_io_edges 0. It has never minted a taint source. serve_forever, uvicorn.run and asyncio.start_server DO fire, so this is per-row, not a language-wide gap. A row can be argued about for an entire panel while minting nothing -- which is why reachability now gates adjudication (WI-vapud) rather than following it.\n\nNO ROW MOVED AND NO OUTPUT CHANGED. ADR-0049 ruling 3 licenses a retag only against a represented crossing (the WI-lunav rule: dropping a row that transmitted nothing, whose every counterpart was catalogued, still collapsed a real violated(3) to inconclusive(0)). TestServerLaunchStaysAReceive remains the pin against a silent split; its docstring is demoted from BEING the ruling to CITING it, which was this item's actual complaint -- the only governing text for a nine-language family was a test docstring.\n\nTHE HALF THAT IS EASY TO OMIT, RECORDED SO IT CANNOT BE. A disclosure boundary MUST be opacity-gating. Since INV-buzab a classified call is what 'examined' means, so a disclosure tag that mints nothing still counts as examined and returns a green tick over live ingress. ADR-0016's own table measures this in the outbound direction: cataloguing curl as net_send moved a cron-dropper claim from inconclusive rc 2 to confirmed rc 0. OPAQUE_BOUNDARIES anticipates the membership in its governing sentence but its EXAMPLE says 'control left this process' -- the outbound instance -- so the extension to the inbound case is made explicitly in the ADR rather than by silent reading.\n\nROOT CAUSE, FILED SEPARATELY AS INV-tafig. io_boundary is absent from _known_axes(): no registry, no axiom, no drift linter, while ADR-0016 and taint.py both call it 'hypergumbo's canonical I/O-boundary risk taxonomy' and four consumers branch on its values. That absence is WHY this question was derived four times and ruled zero times -- there was nowhere a ruling could live. It is the condition under which env_read split (INV-tutar, 134 of 195 rows misclassified, 22.9% precision).\n\nFOLLOW-ONS: WI-hazop (census, blocks the rest) -> WI-vapud (reachability per row) -> WI-nosah (opacity-gated disclosure boundary) + WI-ritog (arrival-scope seeding; start_at:callee, dispatches_to and entrypoints all exist and taint.py references none of them). INV-tafig (declare the axis) is independent and should land before the row moves. INV-kanuk is unblocked by this item reaching a resolved state; note that measurement 0006 already carries an adjudicated verdict against c's socket row, so part of INV-kanuk may fall out of the census directly.\n\nREVERSIBLE. An ADR can be superseded and no catalogue row has moved. If the reachability pass shows the family is largely inert, this becomes a correctness cleanup rather than a precision fix -- which changes its priority, not its verdict."  # 39f3
- op: discuss  # 1f99
  at: '2026-08-30T05:59:37Z'  # 1f99
  by: agent  # 1f99
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 1f99
  clock: 5  # 1f99
  nonce: 1f99  # 1f99
  message: "STEP 3 IS DONE -- measurement 0010, 'Adjudicated deferred-crossing findings, per shape'. This item's ask was one adjudication for the whole family; ADR-0049 gave the ruling and 0010 gives the evidence the ruling's own open work demanded before any row could move.\n\nTHE HEADLINE, AND IT IS NOT WHAT THE SHAPE TABLE PREDICTED. The ruling holds for LAUNCH and fails for HANDLE, and shape is not the discriminator -- MECHANISM is. Two arms over measurement 0006's cohort plus caddy. Arm 1, using 0006's own two-pass labels: the family is 7 situations of 112, 0 TP, 0 useful, against a 33.0% population. Arm 2, a fresh census of 17 repositories on a pinned tree: 50 situations / 411 rows, 14.0% correctness, 14.0% useful. By mechanism: DEFERRED 0 TP of 3, WIRING (cmd.Stdin = os.Stdin) 0 of 8, WRONG-CHANNEL 0 of 17, READ-IN-SCOPE 7 of 22. Three of the four mechanisms produce nothing true at all.\n\nTHE PRE-REGISTERED REFUTATION FIRED SEVEN TIMES. bufio.NewReader(os.Stdin) returns a handle and so fails ruling 1 exactly as ListenAndServe does -- but it is read four lines later in the SAME function, so 'the scope the call does not name' IS the caller's and the mint is not a mis-attribution. Go's entire stdin surface is three deferred-crossing rows (os.Stdin, bufio.NewScanner, bufio.NewReader) and the catalogue has no ReadString / Scan / Text / File.Read to relocate a crossing to, so retagging the HANDLE shape is forbidden by ADR-0049 ruling 3 on its own terms. Filed as WI-vutav: catalogue the reads FIRST.\n\nLAUNCH IS UNANIMOUS AND THIN, and the second half is published as loudly as the first. Every adjudicated launch-family finding in both arms is a false positive -- 2 on the current tree, 4 in 0006 -- and that is ALL the evidence 17 repositories contain. An over-counting grep finds 49 FILES with a launch-shaped call against 2 findings, because jaeger returned inconclusive on all three untrusted-input claims and cilium's framework rows are WI-lalot-inert. So the retag this item asked for is a CORRECTNESS fix worth about two findings, not a precision fix. That changes its priority, not its verdict -- which is what the ADR said it would.\n\nTWO CLAIMS IN THE ADR'S OWN STEP 3 WERE WRONG AND ARE CORRECTED IN PLACE. 0006's corpus does NOT contain caddy (caddy was 0005's, and 0006 excluded 0005's five repositories by construction -- which is exactly why 0006 could call its zero CONFIGURED-ACTION count structural rather than lucky). And it is not true that zero launch-family findings appear among the 112: SEVEN do, three of them the C socket datapoint the same sentence went on to name as the only adjudicated one.\n\nMY OWN FIRST PASS WAS WRONG BY 3.4x AND THE RECORD SAYS SO. Pass 1 read the SOURCE function and accepted the tool's advertised path as evidence a value travelled it; 0001's rubric asks whether the value is an ARGUMENT to the sink call or its RECEIVER, which is a fact about the TERMINAL function of the path. That is the instrument bug measurement 0006 diagnosed in its own packet builder (section F) and I reproduced it by hand. Reading the terminal function took 24 true positives to 7 and 11 'useful' to 7. Both ledgers ship.\n\nREGISTER, LAZY and CALLBACK produced NO findings at all in this cohort. Absence of findings is not evidence of harmlessness, so step 3 is not cleared for them and Phoenix.Router does not move."  # 1f99
- op: discuss  # 1eb8
  at: '2026-08-30T06:47:24Z'  # 1eb8
  by: agent  # 1eb8
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 1eb8
  clock: 6  # 1eb8
  nonce: 1eb8  # 1eb8
  message: "THE RETAG SHIPPED (PR #645, 8aa26f15f5). 28 launch rows -> net_listen in go/python/haskell/erlang; 39 rows now carry the boundary. ADR-0049 open work 1-5 are all discharged.\n\nRULING 3'S PROOF WAS RUN PER LANGUAGE, NOT ARGUED. An idiomatic server probe in each affected language, before and after:\n  go       net_recv 2 [Accept, ListenAndServe]  ->  net_listen 1 + net_recv 1 [Accept]\n  python   net_recv 2 [serve_forever, accept]   ->  net_listen 1 + net_recv 1 [accept]\n  haskell  net_recv 3 [accept, recv, Warp.run]  ->  net_listen 1 + net_recv 2\n  erlang   net_recv 3 [accept, recv, httpd]     ->  net_listen 1 + net_recv 2\nEvery language keeps a net_recv chain afterwards. The finding-level half is in the repo's own suite: four test_taint_recall_corpus.py fixtures used launch rows as their untrusted-input source and were re-pointed at genuine transfers (socket.socket.recv; net.Conn.Read through a DECLARED PARAMETER, the form that resolves), with every assertion holding unchanged.\n\nTWO HOLDOUTS, DIFFERENT REASONS, ASSERTED SEPARATELY -- because one assertion covering both is how the next retag takes the wrong one with it. JavaScript: its whole REACHABLE net_recv surface is {http,https,net}.createServer and Deno.listen, and what would survive is INV-misup-unreachable, so the move would relocate the receive to nothing (F2_EXEMPT's existing reasoning, re-confirmed live by the same probe). Phoenix.Router: 0010 adjudicated NO findings for the REGISTER shape.\n\nTHE PIN INVERTED AND THE ROW EXEMPTION RETIRED ITSELF. TestServerLaunchStaysAReceive is now TestServerLaunchIsADeferredCrossing. F2_EXEMPT_ROWS' fiber App.Listen entry said in terms that the row 'moves with the launch family' and its companion gate was written to fail the moment it did, so this change was FORCED to delete it -- a self-retiring exemption completing its life cycle. The dict stays empty with the mechanism intact, plus a new test asserting POSITIVELY that the row is net_listen now, because an emptied dict is indistinguishable from one emptied to make a red test green.\n\nVERIFIED: 13,414 core tests green (0 failures), 1,321 on the affected language packages. The manifest was hand-extended with the io-boundary surface because smart-test slices on .py and a catalogue-YAML change selects almost nothing.\n\nWHAT IS NOT DONE AND IS NOT THIS ITEM: the HANDLE shape (WI-vutav must catalogue the read calls first -- ruling 3 forbids it until then), the REGISTER/LAZY/CALLBACK shapes (no adjudicated findings), and arrival-scope seeding (WI-ritog), which ADR-0049 explicitly does not authorise."  # 1eb8
